If a contract function accesses the selector of another contracts function like so:

```solidity
contract C {
    function ext_func_call(uint128 amount) public payable {
        A a = new A();
        (bool ok, bytes b) = address(a).call(
            bytes4(A.a.selector) // Here
        );
    }
}

contract A {
    function a() public pure {}
}
```

This will add function `A::a` to contract `C`. The dispatcher dispatches it and it's present in the metadata, which means it gets erroneously added and treated as a contract function further up the compilation pipeline.

I can reproduce this back to Solang `v0.2.0`; earlier versions segfault in `emit`  so it's unclear when this regression was introduced (not recently).

Such snippet
```solidity
contract Contract {
    constructor() {
        assembly {
            return(0, 0)
        }
    }
}
```
can't be parsed due to 
```
 [Diagnostic { level: Error, ty: ParserError, pos: Some(Loc(0, 71, 77)), message: "unrecognised token `return', expected \"address\", \"bool\", \"break\", \"bytes\", \"continue\", \"false\", \"for\", \"if\", \"leave\", \"let\", \"mapping\", \"payable\", \"string\", \"switch\", \"true\", \"}\", Bytes, Int, Uint, hexnumber, identifier, number, string", notes: [] }]
```
error. The reason for this is that `return` is a reserved keyword in Solidity but just a regular function in Yul.

This is an issue similar to https://github.com/hyperledger-labs/solang/pull/636#pullrequestreview-853224787, i.e. requires separation of Yul and Solidity keywords to prevent such collisions.

